(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-cms-exam-5-master-exam-form-c"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 5 Master Exam — Form C","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which description fits recurrent pericarditis?","choices":["Symptoms persist without remission for up to 3 months","Symptoms return after a symptom-free gap of 4 to 6 weeks","Symptoms persist without any remission past 3 months","Constriction persists after an effusion has been drained"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Recurrent pericarditis is defined as symptoms of acute pericarditis returning after a symptom-free interval of 4 to 6 weeks or longer. That symptom-free gap separates it from incessant disease (no remission up to 3 months) and chronic disease (symptoms beyond 3 months). — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman who injects drugs has 5 days of high fever and rigors. Transthoracic imaging shows a large vegetation on the tricuspid valve, and three sets of blood cultures are drawn from separate sites. Which organism are the cultures most likely to grow?","choices":["Candida albicans","Viridans streptococci","Staphylococcus aureus","Enterococcus faecalis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In people who inject drugs, Staphylococcus aureus, a skin organism, causes more than 60% of all endocarditis and 80-90% of cases in which the tricuspid valve is infected. Her 5 days of high fever and rigors with a large tricuspid vegetation fit that pattern. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy has chest pressure when climbing stairs. Coronary angiography performed before a planned myectomy shows normal coronary arteries. Which explanation best accounts for his exertional chest pain?","choices":["Unrecognized coronary plaque rupture","Outflow obstruction and thick muscle demand","A dilated, thin-walled ventricle","Inflammation of the pericardium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Angina in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy occurs in the absence of coronary artery disease, as his normal angiogram confirms. It comes from left ventricular outflow tract obstruction and increased myocardial oxygen consumption by the hypertrophied ventricle, causing ischemia, especially during exertion such as climbing stairs. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman is brought in by her daughter for 2 days of new confusion. She has a fever of 38.9 °C (102.0 °F), a new murmur and conjunctival petechiae (pinpoint hemorrhages on the conjunctiva). Brain imaging shows multiple small scattered lesions without a large stroke. Which mechanism best explains her confusion?","choices":["A side effect of sedation","Septic cerebral emboli","A primary bacterial meningitis","Low-flow ischemia from heart block"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fever, a new murmur and conjunctival petechiae suggest endocarditis, and multiple small scattered brain lesions fit septic emboli. Cerebral complications include ischemic stroke, abscess, intracranial hemorrhage and meningitis, and neurologic deficits are a recognized manifestation of endocarditis. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which organism is a fungal cause of pericarditis?","choices":["Coxsackievirus","Mycoplasma","Echinococcus","Histoplasma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Histoplasma is a listed fungal cause of pericarditis, along with Aspergillus, Blastomyces, Coccidioides and Candida. Infectious causes are grouped as viral, bacterial, fungal and parasitic, and most idiopathic cases are thought to be viral. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with a long QT has recurrent runs of torsades de pointes (QRS complexes that twist around the baseline). Between runs he is alert, with a pulse and a normal blood pressure. Which is the first treatment?","choices":["Intravenous magnesium","Intravenous procainamide","Intravenous amiodarone","Intravenous metoprolol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Between runs he is alert with a pulse and a normal blood pressure, so his torsades de pointes is treated with intravenous magnesium to break it, followed by more magnesium as an infusion. Amiodarone, procainamide and beta blockers are avoided; lidocaine is the one antiarrhythmic that can be used. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with hypertension has an acute aortic dissection with severe tearing chest pain and a blood pressure of 176/100. Which intravenous medication should be started to lower her pressure?","choices":["Esmolol","Heparin","Cilostazol","Alteplase"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her acute dissection with a pressure of 176/100 needs progression stopped by reducing blood pressure, with a systolic target of 100 to 120 mmHg. The drugs named are a beta blocker such as esmolol or a vasodilator such as nitroprusside, given with pain management before surgical grafting. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 51, 66, 67, 118"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has a continuous bruit and a palpable thrill (a vibration felt over the skin) over the left femoral region, with mild leg swelling. He has not had dialysis and has no trauma history. His only recent procedure was an endovascular repair of an iliac artery performed through the left groin. Which is the most likely cause?","choices":["Progressive superficial venous reflux","Surgical creation for dialysis access","Groin access for the procedure","Congenital vascular malformation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A bruit and thrill over the left femoral region after an endovascular repair performed through the left groin point to an iatrogenic arteriovenous (AV) fistula. Lower extremity fistulas are most commonly caused by groin access for endovascular procedures. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"What is the aim of cardiac rehabilitation programs in cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Screening of relatives for gene defects","Better exercise tolerance and quality of life","Replacement of all needed medications","Reversal of thickened ventricular walls"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac rehabilitation programs aim to improve exercise tolerance and quality of life. Rehabilitative care also includes education on medication adherence, nutrition and symptom monitoring, psychological support for coping with chronic illness, and home-based options for elderly or mobility-limited patients. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"A genitourinary source of bacteremia points to which endocarditis organism?","choices":["Staphylococci","Haemophilus species","Enterococci","Viridans streptococci"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Enterococci come from the genitourinary tract, so a urinary source of bacteremia points to them. Enterococci appear among both acute and subacute causes and are a recognized cause of native valve endocarditis. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has fatigue, exertional dyspnea, an elevated jugular venous pressure with the Kussmaul sign (a rise on inspiration), ascites and minimal ankle edema. His liver enzymes are mildly raised. Which condition is the key noncardiac mimic to consider?","choices":["Asthma","Hyperthyroidism","Pneumonia","Cirrhosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cirrhosis is the noncardiac condition in the differential of constrictive pericarditis, alongside subacute tamponade and restrictive cardiomyopathy. His ascites with little ankle edema and mildly raised liver enzymes could mislead toward the liver; echocardiographic features such as increased pericardial thickness and septal bounce help confirm constriction. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman has an asymptomatic abdominal aortic aneurysm measuring 5.3 cm on computed tomography. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":["Ultrasound every three years","Repair only once it ruptures","Elective repair","Imaging every 6 to 12 months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Elective repair is advised at 5.0 cm or larger in women, compared with over 5.5 cm in men, so her asymptomatic 5.3 cm aneurysm qualifies. Her sex is the decisive clue: the same diameter in a man would still be in the surveillance range. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man had a plaster cast removed from his right lower leg two days ago after a tibial fracture. He now has right calf swelling that measures 4 cm more than the left, and tenderness along the deep veins of the calf. Nothing else on the Wells criteria is present and no alternative diagnosis is as likely. What is his Wells score?","choices":["One point","Two points","Three points","Four points"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Recent plaster immobilization of the lower extremity 1 + calf swelling 4 cm more than the other leg (over 3 cm) 1 + localized tenderness along the deep venous system 1 = 3. No alternative diagnosis is as likely, so nothing is subtracted; a score of 3 or greater means a high clinical likelihood of deep vein thrombosis (DVT). — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman has her second recurrence of idiopathic pericarditis despite colchicine. Her temperature is 38.4 degrees Celsius, C-reactive protein is 45 mg/L, and cardiac magnetic resonance shows pericardial edema with late gadolinium enhancement. Which therapy is most appropriate to add?","choices":["Anakinra","Intravenous immunoglobulin","Azathioprine","Pericardiectomy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A second recurrence of idiopathic pericarditis despite colchicine, with a temperature of 38.4 degrees Celsius, a C-reactive protein of 45 mg/L (above 10) and pericardial inflammation on magnetic resonance, is the inflammatory phenotype. An interleukin-1 blocker such as anakinra or rilonacept is added and is preferred for colchicine-resistant recurrence. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man presents with new-onset heart failure a week after a flulike illness. He smokes and has hyperlipidemia, so the team wants to exclude coronary disease before labeling this myocarditis. Which test rules out coronary ischemia as the cause of his new heart failure?","choices":["Chest radiograph","Sedimentation rate","Cardiac angiography","Endomyocardial biopsy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac angiography is used in the myocarditis work-up to rule out coronary ischemia as a cause of new-onset heart failure. That matters for this man, whose smoking and hyperlipidemia give him coronary risk, before his heart failure after a flulike illness is labeled myocarditis. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with chronic kidney disease has native valve endocarditis from penicillin-susceptible viridans streptococci. The team is choosing his antibiotic plan. Which choice should be avoided?","choices":["The 2-week gentamicin regimen","Monitoring renal function","Penicillin G for the full course","Ceftriaxone for the full course"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The 2-week regimen that adds gentamicin is reserved for uncomplicated endocarditis with a rapid response to therapy and no chronic kidney disease. His chronic kidney disease excludes it, so he is treated with a directed agent such as penicillin G, ceftriaxone or vancomycin without that shortcut. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with a large anterior myocardial infarction two years ago has palpitations and lightheadedness. He is alert, with a blood pressure of 112/70. The monitor shows a regular wide QRS rhythm at 170 per minute with no P waves, every complex the same shape, for over a minute. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Ventricular fibrillation","Monomorphic ventricular tachycardia","Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia","Complete heart block"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A regular wide rhythm at 170 with no P waves and every complex the same shape is monomorphic ventricular tachycardia, and lasting over a minute makes it sustained. Monomorphic tachycardia is often seen with heart disease such as a prior myocardial infarction; he is stable, so drug cardioversion fits. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with aortic valve endocarditis has a transesophageal echocardiogram that shows an abscess around the valve ring. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Repeat echo in 6 months","Oral step-down now","Shorten antibiotics","Surgical evaluation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An abscess around the valve ring is an indication for surgery, alongside heart failure from valvular dysfunction (the most common indication), infection not resolving with appropriate antibiotics and fungal infection. Cardiothoracic surgery is consulted to evaluate her for surgery. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has Wenckebach block (a PR interval that grows longer until a beat is dropped) found on a routine tracing. She feels well and asks what would make her a candidate for a pacemaker. Which answer is most accurate?","choices":["Symptoms such as fainting","Dropped beats during sleep","A PR above 0.20 seconds","Being over 65 years old"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mobitz type I (Wenckebach) is usually asymptomatic and needs no treatment unless symptoms are debilitating. A permanent pacemaker is considered for symptomatic patients, such as those with dizziness, fatigue, exertional intolerance or syncope, usually when exertion induces the block. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"In unstable Mobitz type II block, when medications are not used or do not work, which is the emergent measure?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Vagal maneuvers","Defibrillation","Transcutaneous pacing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Unstable Mobitz type II is treated emergently with atropine or epinephrine (both no longer contraindicated but highly controversial) or transcutaneous pacing. When medications are not used or do not work, transcutaneous pacing is the emergent measure, bridging the patient to the definitive permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Stable atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response is treated how?","choices":["Intravenous rate control","Synchronized cardioversion","Immediate ablation","Intravenous atropine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hemodynamically stable atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response is treated with intravenous rate control. Without rapid ventricular response, rate control alone is used, and anticoagulation is part of management for all; instability changes the answer to synchronized cardioversion. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man in atrial flutter has a heart rate of 150, a blood pressure of 70/40, and is confused. Which is the next step?","choices":["Oral rate control","Anticoagulate for 21 days","Vagal maneuver","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Atrial flutter at 150 with a blood pressure of 70/40 and confusion is hemodynamically unstable, and unstable atrial flutter is treated with synchronized cardioversion. Flutter usually does not cause hemodynamic collapse, so instability like this calls for a prompt shock. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman admitted with stress cardiomyopathy after a house fire has a repeat echocardiogram that shows a thrombus in the akinetic left ventricular apex. She is in sinus rhythm. Which treatment is now indicated?","choices":["Nitrate infusion","Pacemaker insertion","Anticoagulation","Surgical myectomy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Treatment of Takotsubo cardiomyopathy is empiric and supportive and depends on the presentation, and a thrombus in her akinetic left ventricular apex is the finding that calls for anticoagulation. Beta blockers are helpful, and diuretics are added only if heart failure is present; most patients completely recover. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman had a peripherally inserted central catheter placed in her right arm two weeks ago for long-term antibiotic therapy. She reports aching and swelling of the arm. Examination shows diffuse swelling of the entire right upper arm with mild redness, and there is no palpable cord and no purulent drainage. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Venous duplex ultrasound of the arm","Warm compresses and ibuprofen","Oral antibiotics for cellulitis","Reassurance and arm elevation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Upper extremity deep vein thrombosis (DVT) often presents only with limb swelling and arm discomfort, which is her picture: diffuse swelling of the whole upper arm two weeks after a catheter was placed. With no cord or purulence to suggest a superficial process, imaging the arm veins with duplex ultrasound confirms or excludes the clot. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman with varicose veins has a small, tender, red cord along the great saphenous vein of the calf. Venous duplex ultrasound confirms a short segment of superficial clot. She has no other risk factors for thrombosis. Which is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Inferior vena cava filter placement","Broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics","Warm compresses and ibuprofen","Catheter-directed thrombolysis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Her clot is a short superficial segment, she has no other thrombotic risk factors, and superficial thrombophlebitis rarely embolizes or causes serious complications. Typical treatment is warm compresses, compression, elevation and a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) such as ibuprofen, with anticoagulation kept for extensive clot or higher risk. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man has a regular narrow complex tachycardia at 190 with hidden P waves. He is alert with a blood pressure of 128/80. A properly performed vagal maneuver does not change the rhythm. Which is the next step?","choices":["Intravenous magnesium","Adenosine","Atropine","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. He is stable, alert with a blood pressure of 128/80, and his regular narrow tachycardia at 190 with hidden P waves has not responded to a proper vagal maneuver. Adenosine is next and may be repeated once; it may convert the rhythm to normal sinus rhythm or slow it enough to show whether P waves are there. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman is recovering in hospital from stress cardiomyopathy that followed news of her son's serious illness. Her chest pain has resolved, and she asks whether her heart will stay damaged. Which answer about her prognosis is most accurate?","choices":["Most patients recover completely","It always progresses to failure","Poorest outlook of any type","Half die within five years"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Most patients with Takotsubo cardiomyopathy completely recover. Care in hospital is empiric and supportive, with beta blockers, anticoagulation if a left ventricular thrombus is present and diuretics if heart failure is present, so she can be told her heart is expected to recover. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman has inappropriate sinus tachycardia at about 110 after a full workup excluded other causes. Her only symptom is awareness of her heartbeat, and she asks whether she needs medication. Which response is most accurate?","choices":["She needs a lifelong beta blocker","She needs anticoagulation","Usually left untreated; benign","Cardioversion will cure it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Inappropriate sinus tachycardia is generally left untreated because patients tolerate the symptoms better than the treatment and the prognosis is usually benign. Management centers on removing influencing factors, such as night-shift work, illicit drugs and medications, rather than on beta blockers, which are poorly tolerated. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman with suspected tamponade undergoes right heart catheterization before drainage. Which pressure finding is expected?","choices":["An isolated high left atrial pressure","Low right atrial pressure","Equal pressures in all chambers","A gradient across the aortic valve"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac catheterization in tamponade shows equalization of pressures in all chambers of the heart. Elevated intrapericardial pressure compresses filling of every chamber, which also explains the greatly elevated jugular venous pressure and the venous waveform with a prominent x descent and absent y descent. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman has episodes of lightheadedness and one faint. A 24-hour ambulatory monitor shows runs of a fast, irregular rhythm that alternate with slow rates and long pauses. Which fast rhythm is most likely on her monitor?","choices":["Atrioventricular nodal reentry","Atrial fibrillation","Sinus tachycardia","Ventricular fibrillation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Runs of a fast, irregular rhythm alternating with slow rates and long pauses, with lightheadedness and a faint, are tachy-brady syndrome, a form of sick sinus syndrome. The tachyarrhythmia in tachy-brady syndrome is most commonly atrial fibrillation, which fits an irregular fast run. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Why is renal function checked in peripheral artery disease?","choices":["To measure homocysteine","Risk of kidney stones","To grade the claudication","Risk of renal insufficiency"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Patients with peripheral artery disease are at risk for renal insufficiency, so renal function is part of the work-up, alongside an electrocardiogram, complete blood count, lipid profile, metabolic panel, fasting glucose and hemoglobin A1c, which reflects average glucose over three months. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with long-standing hypertension has a routine electrocardiogram. The QRS is 0.16 seconds wide, with a dominant S wave in V1, a broad single-peaked R wave in leads I, V5 and V6, and no Q waves in those leads. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Left bundle branch block","Right bundle branch block","Ventricular tachycardia","First-degree block"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A QRS over 0.12 seconds (here 0.16) with a dominant S wave in V1, a broad monophasic R in I, V5 and V6 and no Q waves in those leads is left bundle branch block. The left ventricle is activated by current crossing the septum from the right, and chronic hypertension, his long-standing condition, is a named cause. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman with dilated cardiomyopathy also has enlarged hilar lymph nodes and skin nodules, and an infiltrative process such as sarcoidosis is suspected. Coronary angiography showed no disease. Which imaging test best identifies an inflammatory or infiltrative cause?","choices":["Ambulatory rhythm monitoring for forty-eight hours","Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging with gadolinium","A repeat resting electrocardiogram in six months","Cardiac catheterization with pressure tracings"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sarcoidosis is suspected, and cardiac magnetic resonance imaging with gadolinium contrast is helpful in identifying inflammatory, ischemic or infiltrative processes such as sarcoidosis or hemochromatosis in dilated cardiomyopathy. Endocardial biopsy is rarely useful but may be considered for systemic disease with possible cardiac involvement. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man whose Wolff-Parkinson-White was found on a school electrocardiogram presents with palpitations. The rhythm is irregular and wide-complex, at rates up to 260, with varying delta waves (slurred upstrokes). He is alert with a blood pressure of 118/74. Which drug is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Intravenous procainamide","Atropine","Intravenous magnesium","Adenosine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An irregular, wide-complex tachycardia up to 260 with varying delta waves in a man with known Wolff-Parkinson-White is atrial fibrillation conducting over the accessory pathway. When Wolff-Parkinson-White is known, intravenous procainamide is used, never adenosine; he is alert with a normal blood pressure, so a drug is appropriate. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man had 20 minutes of weakness and numbness in his right hand four months ago that fully resolved. Duplex ultrasonography shows 75% stenosis of the left internal carotid artery. A friend with no symptoms was told surgery waits until stenosis passes 80%, and he asks whether the same applies to him. Which response is accurate?","choices":["Yes; resolved symptoms do not count","No; over 70% already qualifies him","Yes; surgery waits until over 80%","No; he needs medical therapy only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. His 20 minutes of hand weakness and numbness was a transient ischemic attack (a focal loss of neurologic function lasting under 24 hours) within the last six months, so he is symptomatic. Surgery is advised for symptomatic stenosis over 70%, which his 75% meets; the over-80% rule is for asymptomatic patients. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 72, 75"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man with acute pericarditis says the pain keeps him awake because it is worse when he lies down. Which advice is most appropriate?","choices":["Keep the head of the bed elevated","Sleep prone to splint the chest","Sleep flat to rest the heart","Take an extra dose at bedtime"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pericardial pain is worse when supine, which is why lying down keeps him awake, and keeping the head of the bed elevated is part of follow-up counseling. That advice sits with adherence to the full treatment course, activity restriction and recognizing red-flag symptoms. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Where do atherosclerotic lesions most often form?","choices":["In the smallest arterioles of the skin","In straight segments with laminar flow","In the veins draining the lower legs","At branch points and turbulent sites"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Atherosclerotic lesions often form at arterial bifurcations (branching) and at sites of increased turbulence, and they develop over many decades. Plaque can lead to stenosis, thrombus can form at the plaque site, and atheroemboli can originate from it. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"When are palliative care discussions raised for elderly patients with cardiomyopathy?","choices":["At the first visit","Never in the elderly","After transplant only","In advanced stages"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. For elderly patients with cardiomyopathy, palliative care discussions are held in advanced stages, alongside therapy tailored to comorbidities and frailty, cautious use of diuretics and vasodilators, and a focus on symptom control and quality of life. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman has constant throbbing, burning pain in her toes and forefoot that is worse lying flat, not relieved by narcotic pain medicine, and eased by dangling her leg or walking around. Her feet are cool. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Intermittent claudication","Peripheral neuropathy","Acute limb ischemia","Critical limb ischemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ischemic rest pain of critical limb ischemia is constant, throbbing or burning, in the forefoot and toes, with cool feet. It worsens with elevation or reclining, is not readily controlled even by narcotics, and is relieved by dependency or walking around, which use gravity to aid perfusion. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 19, 21, 23, 40, 59"},{"q":"Which acute event can cause right bundle branch block by suddenly raising right ventricular pressure?","choices":["Hypertensive crisis","Aortic dissection","Pulmonary embolism","Acute gastroenteritis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A sudden increase in right ventricular pressure, as from a pulmonary embolism, is a named cause of right bundle branch block. The chronic counterpart is pulmonary hypertension, and other causes include degeneration, ischemic heart disease and cardiomyopathies; treatment depends on the underlying condition. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with right-sided heart failure, ascites and a raised jugular venous pressure has cardiac magnetic resonance imaging to decide between restrictive cardiomyopathy and constrictive pericarditis. Which imaging finding would point to constrictive pericarditis instead?","choices":["Preserved ejection fraction","Normal ventricular cavity size","Pericardial thickening","Marked atrial enlargement"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Echocardiography alone cannot separate restrictive cardiomyopathy from constrictive pericarditis, so cardiac magnetic resonance imaging is used, and pericardial thickening points to pericarditis. Big atria, a preserved ejection fraction and normal cavity size are restrictive features that do not decide the question. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with previous infective endocarditis needs a root canal. She developed hives (urticaria) after amoxicillin years ago and can take pills. Which prophylaxis is most appropriate?","choices":["Oral amoxicillin","Oral cephalexin","Oral azithromycin","Oral clindamycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Previous endocarditis qualifies her, and a root canal manipulates the periapical region of the tooth, so she needs prophylaxis. She can take pills but had urticaria with amoxicillin, which rules out penicillins and cephalexin, leaving azithromycin or clarithromycin 500 mg (or doxycycline) 30-60 minutes before. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman in torsades de pointes (a polymorphic rhythm whose QRS complexes twist around the baseline) becomes unresponsive with a blood pressure of 60/30 and only a faint pulse. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Defibrillation","Intravenous lidocaine","Transcutaneous pacing","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Unresponsive with a blood pressure of 60/30, she is hemodynamically unstable, and an unstable patient in torsades de pointes is defibrillated (unsynchronized cardioversion). Magnesium is the drug for a conscious patient, and amiodarone, procainamide and beta blockers are avoided because they prolong the QT. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man has had 6 weeks of low-grade fever and weight loss and has a new murmur. Subacute endocarditis is suspected. Which complete blood count finding is most likely?","choices":["Eosinophilia","Normocytic anemia","Microcytic anemia","Polycythemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Six weeks of low-grade fever, weight loss and a new murmur suggest subacute endocarditis, and the complete blood count with differential shows leukocytosis and anemia of chronic disease with a normal mean corpuscular volume, which is a normocytic anemia. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman with a normal pulse examination and ankle-brachial index has had her venous ulcer heal after months of compression. She asks why it opened again after she bumped her leg. Which feature of a healed venous ulcer explains this?","choices":["Fragile thin scar tissue","Undertreated deep infection","Lymphatic channel obstruction","Persistent arterial ischemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A venous ulcer heals with a thin scar that can break down easily with minor trauma or increased leg edema. Her bump to the leg is exactly that kind of minor trauma, so the reopening reflects the fragile scar rather than a new disease process. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with known atrioventricular nodal reentry has a regular narrow complex tachycardia at 178. She is stable. Which treatment almost always terminates it?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Catheter ablation","Intravenous procainamide","Vagal maneuvers or adenosine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. She is stable with a regular narrow tachycardia at 178 and known nodal reentry, and atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia almost always terminates with vagal maneuvers or adenosine. An educated patient whose vagal maneuver works at home usually does not need urgent medical attention. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man has a moderate-to-large pericardial effusion on echocardiogram with no hemodynamic compromise. Viral, uremic, thyroid and rheumatologic workups are unrevealing. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Start empiric anakinra","Immediate pericardiectomy","Diagnostic pericardiocentesis","Reassure and discharge"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A moderate-to-large effusion whose viral, uremic, thyroid and rheumatologic workups are unrevealing is an effusion of unknown etiology, an indication for diagnostic pericardiocentesis. The fluid is sent for cell count, Gram stain, bacterial and fungal culture, acid-fast stain with mycobacterial culture, and cytology. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with extensive atherosclerosis develops painful, tender, bluish discoloration of several toes on his left foot, with localized pallor. His dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses are easily palpable. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Atheroembolism","Acute embolic limb ischemia","Dry gangrene","Critical limb ischemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Atheroemboli of fibrin, platelets and cholesterol debris from proximal plaques lodge in small vessels of muscle and skin, causing blue toe syndrome with localized tenderness, pallor and livedo reticularis. Because large vessels may not be occluded, distal pulses usually remain palpable, as his are. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 23-year-old previously healthy man has breathlessness, chest pain and exercise intolerance ten days after a flulike illness with fever, myalgia and diarrhea. He is tachycardic with a third heart sound (an early diastolic sound just after the second heart sound) and crackles at both bases. Troponin is elevated and coronary angiography is normal. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Takotsubo cardiomyopathy","Acute viral myocarditis","Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A previously healthy young man with heart failure symptoms ten days after fever, myalgia and diarrhea fits viral myocarditis, which follows a recent (1 to 2 week) flulike syndrome; young males are particularly susceptible. His tachycardia, third heart sound and crackles match the examination. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is admitted with an exacerbation and respiratory failure. His pulse is 128 and irregular. The electrocardiogram shows a P wave before every QRS, with at least three different P-wave shapes in lead II. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Atrial fibrillation","Multifocal atrial tachycardia","Atrial flutter, variable block","Focal atrial tachycardia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An irregular rhythm at 128 with a P wave before every QRS and at least three distinct P-wave shapes in lead II is multifocal atrial tachycardia, from multiple atrial foci. It typically occurs in acutely ill patients with severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease or heart failure, as with his exacerbation and respiratory failure. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman with a malar rash, painful joints and a positive antinuclear antibody test has a new murmur. She has no fever, repeated blood cultures are negative, and echocardiography shows small sterile-appearing valve vegetations. Which is the most likely cause of her valve lesion?","choices":["Subacute infective endocarditis","Device endocarditis","Acute infective endocarditis","Libman-Sacks endocarditis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. She has features of systemic lupus erythematosus (malar rash, painful joints, positive antinuclear antibody), and lupus may present with non-infective Libman-Sacks endocarditis. No fever, repeatedly negative blood cultures and sterile-appearing vegetations fit this autoimmune mimic rather than infection. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman in ventricular fibrillation arrest has received shocks, epinephrine and amiodarone, and she remains in ventricular fibrillation. Which drug may be considered next?","choices":["Metoprolol","Adenosine","Lidocaine","Verapamil"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. She has had shocks, epinephrine and amiodarone and is still in ventricular fibrillation, so a lidocaine push may be considered for rhythm refractory to shock after the antiarrhythmic. Calcium gluconate and sodium bicarbonate may also be considered while cardiopulmonary resuscitation continues. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with a known ascending thoracic aortic aneurysm develops a new diastolic murmur, exertional dyspnea and leg swelling. Which complication does the aneurysm's location explain?","choices":["Esophageal compression","Aortic regurgitation","Tracheal compression","Recurrent laryngeal palsy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An ascending thoracic aortic aneurysm can cause aortic regurgitation and heart failure. That explains his new diastolic murmur, exertional dyspnea and leg swelling, while arch aneurysms cause hoarseness and descending ones cause compression symptoms. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"In the low likelihood Wells category, what happens after a negative D-dimer?","choices":["Duplex ultrasound follows","Venography is performed","The test is repeated in a week","The work-up stops"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In the low-likelihood Wells category (score zero or less), a negative D-dimer ends the work-up. The test is sensitive, so a negative result reliably rules out deep vein thrombosis (DVT) in these patients, while a positive one would lead to ultrasound. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with a pacemaker implanted two weeks ago has three days of swelling and aching of the left arm. The pacemaker lead has injured the wall of the subclavian vein. Which component of the Virchow triad does this injury represent?","choices":["Hypercoagulable state","Endothelial trauma","Venous stasis","Impaired lymphatic drainage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lead has physically injured the wall of the subclavian vein, which is damage to the vessel lining: endothelial trauma. Pacemakers, internal cardiac defibrillators and indwelling central venous catheters are the most common causes of upper-extremity deep vein thrombosis (DVT), which makes up only 5% to 10% of all DVTs. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 37-year-old man was treated for acute pericarditis 4 weeks ago and now feels well. A repeat electrocardiogram shows widespread T-wave inversions without ST elevation, and troponin is normal. Which best explains the tracing?","choices":["Expected evolution of the illness","A new silent myocardial infarction","Developing cardiac tamponade","Hyperkalemia from colchicine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Four weeks after acute pericarditis, in a man who feels well with a normal troponin, widespread T-wave inversion without ST elevation is stage 3 of the classic evolution. ST elevation with PR depression comes first, then ST normalization with T-wave flattening at 1 to 3 weeks, then inversion from 3 weeks on, and finally a normal tracing. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man admitted with pneumonia develops frequent early, wide QRS beats with no preceding P wave on telemetry, and he feels his heart skipping. His oxygen saturation on the monitor is drifting down. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Start intravenous amiodarone","Immediate defibrillation","Refer for catheter ablation","Check blood gas, electrolytes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Early, wide beats with no P wave are premature ventricular contractions, and hypoxemia and electrolyte imbalance (hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, hypercalcemia) are the main causes. With pneumonia and a drifting oxygen saturation, checking a blood gas and electrolytes, then correcting what is found, comes first. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"An 80-year-old man is found on the floor two days after collapsing. His right leg has profound sensory loss, paralysis with rigid muscles, and no arterial or venous Doppler signal. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Urgent computed tomography angiography","Catheter-directed thrombolysis","Surgical thromboembolectomy","Major amputation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Profound sensory loss, paralysis with rigid muscles and inaudible arterial and venous Doppler signals define an irreversibly ischemic limb with major tissue loss or permanent nerve damage. Nonviable extremities require major amputation regardless of the therapy instituted. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What is the first-line treatment for acute, symptomatic, hemodynamically unstable bradycardia?","choices":["A permanent pacemaker","Adenosine","Amiodarone","Atropine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acute, symptomatic, hemodynamically unstable bradycardia is treated first with atropine. If it fails, a temporary pacemaker follows; chronic symptomatic bradycardia is managed with a permanent pacemaker, and asymptomatic bradycardia is not treated at all. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man has a first proximal deep vein thrombosis with no surgery, trauma, hormone use, travel, cancer or other identifiable trigger, and no inherited disorder has been found. How long should anticoagulation continue?","choices":["Three to six months","Indefinitely","Six weeks","Three months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. His first proximal deep vein thrombosis had no surgery, trauma, hormones, travel, cancer or other trigger, which makes it an unprovoked first venous thromboembolism (VTE). An unprovoked first VTE is treated for an indefinite length of time, beyond the 3-month minimum that applies to every first episode. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 64"}]};
